# An associated-effect projection **in a parameter's row**, which is where the
# projection's evidence slot is actually read rather than merely forwarded.
#
#     fn via[T: Runner](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !T.E) -> Int !T.E = run(x) + t()
#
# `assoc_effects.dawn` writes its projections in *return* rows, and a return
# row is the one position that never walks the slot: the callee forwards it to
# a dictionary and a devirtualised impl casts it back out. Put the projection
# on a parameter and the slot reaches a reader -- `t` is a function value, the
# call hands it that slot as its pack, and the closure inside asks the pack for
# a label's node. A pack answers; the bare evidence *record* the call site used
# to put there answers nothing, so the program checked clean and panicked with
# `types.EV_MISS_MSG` (#355). The alias spelling forwards the same row and so
# forwarded the same hole (#351), which is why `via_alias` is here beside the
# inline twin rather than in a corpus of its own.
#
# The repair is that a projection's slot carries a **pack**, the way an effect
# variable's does and the way `lower.ev_from_pack` has always said it does. So
# the shapes below are chosen for what walks the slot:
#
#   * the two spellings of the parameter position, inline and through an alias;
#   * two subjects binding the member to two different labels, so one generic
#     signature is instantiated at both in the same program;
#   * a **named function value** crossing into the projection's slot, where the
#     question is assignability rather than inference;
#   * the projection's pack carried into a **primitive's** closure argument,
#     which is #346's reader over #355's slot;
#   * the empty pack, from a binding that is pure and from one that is io --
#     the controls, since a closure that reads nothing cannot tell a lost pack
#     from a kept one;
#   * a frame that **forwards** the projection without grounding it, so what
#     travels is one pack through two signatures;
#   * a written label beside the projection in the same parameter row, which is
#     a ground node consed over the projection's pack;
#   * a trait **default body** reached through the projection, which is the
#     dictionary-bridge reader rather than the devirtualised one.
#
# Three more because the atom's *neighbours* decide how the failure showed. One
# projection alone took the allocation-free path -- the slot went over as the
# pack -- and missed, so the report was `EV_MISS_MSG`. A projection beside a
# second evidence atom is consed or joined instead, the record ends up in a
# chain's `outer` field or under `ev_append`, and what came back was another
# atom's record: a `ClassCastException` rather than a panic, on a program that
# also checked clean. `!io` is not an atom and pays for nothing, so it is the
# one neighbour that never triggered either. So:
#
#   * a label beside the projection **crossing an effect variable's slot**, at
#     a callee that takes `fn() -> Int !e` -- the shape that reported a cast;
#   * two projections in one row, on two traits;
#   * two projections **on one trait**, read from inside a lambda. Both slots
#     key on `types.assoc_key`, which bands by trait id, so this is where a
#     collision would show. It answers because a non-label key is answered by
#     the chain as a whole and a superset of a pack is a pack -- the same
#     property the design leans on everywhere else. Before the repair it
#     answered one projection with the *other's* record.
#
# And the half of that last one it does not reach. `two_subjects` puts two
# projections of one trait in a row by taking **two subjects** -- `T.E` and
# `U.E`, one member, two impls. A trait may declare more than one member, and
# then `T.E` and `T.F` are two atoms of one row on **one** subject, filled from
# **one** impl, addressed by one `assoc_key` band. That is the narrowest case
# the band has: two members named apart in the source and not apart in the key.
# Three shapes for it, all on a trait whose second subject binds the two
# members the other way round, so a slot answered by the trait's id rather than
# by the label its binding produced hands one line the other's handler and says
# so in the arithmetic:
#
#   * both members read inside a lambda a callee applies;
#   * both members through `list.fold`, whose `!e` is a std signature's
#     parameter row rather than one this file wrote;
#   * one member handed out as a **function value's** row while the frame reads
#     the other itself, so the two slots of one band are split between a
#     callee and this frame.
#
# And a trait whose member carries a **default** row (#369), taken by one
# subject and overridden by the other, because materialization promises a
# defaulted impl is the explicit one's byte-twin and a promise about evidence
# is this file's to keep.
#
# And the control the whole file needs: a projection in a **return** row, which
# is what `assoc_effects.dawn` is made of and what worked all along.
#
# **Every line installs its own handlers and every handler answers a different
# prime**, for the reason effect_widen_row.dawn states: shared handlers at the
# top of `main` let one correctly built pack seed every other line's read, and
# a repair that fixed one call site in eight would print eight right answers.
use std/io
use std/list

effect AskE {
  fn ask() -> Int
}

effect TellE {
  fn tell() -> Int
}

effect LogE {
  fn log() -> Int
}

trait Runner[T] {
  effect E
  fn run(x: T) -> Int !T.E
  fn twice(x: T) -> Int !T.E = run(x) + run(x)
}

## A second trait with an effect member of its own, so that a row can hold two
## projections whose keys come from two different trait ids.
trait Other[U] {
  effect F
  fn other(y: U) -> Int !U.F
}

## A trait with **two** effect members. `Runner` has one, so every row above
## that holds two projections gets the second from another trait or another
## subject; this is the one that gets it from another *member*, which is the
## pair `types.assoc_key` cannot tell apart -- the band is `3 * trait_id + 2`
## and the member name is not in it.
trait Pair[T] {
  effect E
  effect F
  fn lo(x: T) -> Int !T.E
  fn hi(x: T) -> Int !T.F
}

## A trait whose member carries a **default** row (#369). Registration
## materializes the default into the impl that writes no binding, so a
## defaulted impl must be indistinguishable from an explicitly bound one all
## the way down: same reduction, same slot, same pack. `ByDefault` leans on
## the default and `Overr` overrides it with the *other* label, so a
## materialization that ignores the declared row, or a default applied over
## a written binding, hands one of the two lines the other's handler and the
## arithmetic says which.
trait Dflt[T] {
  effect E = !AskE
  fn deft(x: T) -> Int !T.E
}

type One = { v: Int }

type Two = { v: Int }

type Quiet = { v: Int }

type Loud = { v: Int }

type Ping = { v: Int }

type Pong = { v: Int }

impl Runner[One] {
  effect E = !AskE
  fn run(x: One) -> Int !AskE = x.v + ask()
}

## A second subject binding the member to a **different** label, so one
## generic `via` is instantiated at two labels in the same program. A slot that
## answered by position rather than by key, or a pack shared between the two
## instantiations, gives one of these two lines the other's handler.
impl Runner[Two] {
  effect E = !TellE
  fn run(x: Two) -> Int !TellE = x.v + tell()
}

impl Other[Two] {
  effect F = !LogE
  fn other(y: Two) -> Int !LogE = y.v + log()
}

impl Runner[Quiet] {
  effect E = !()
  fn run(x: Quiet) -> Int = x.v
}

impl Runner[Loud] {
  effect E = !io
  fn run(x: Loud) -> Int !io = {
    println("loud")
    x.v
  }
}

## The two impls of `Pair` bind its two members to the same two labels the
## other way round. One generic consumer is instantiated at both, so a slot
## that answered by trait id alone -- or a pack shared between the members --
## does not merely lose a handler, it swaps two that are both installed, and
## the arithmetic below is what says which happened.
impl Pair[Ping] {
  effect E = !AskE
  effect F = !TellE
  fn lo(x: Ping) -> Int !AskE = x.v + ask()
  fn hi(x: Ping) -> Int !TellE = x.v + tell()
}

impl Pair[Pong] {
  effect E = !TellE
  effect F = !AskE
  fn lo(x: Pong) -> Int !TellE = x.v + tell()
  fn hi(x: Pong) -> Int !AskE = x.v + ask()
}

type ByDefault = { v: Int }

type Overr = { v: Int }

impl Dflt[ByDefault] {
  fn deft(x: ByDefault) -> Int !AskE = x.v + ask()
}

impl Dflt[Overr] {
  effect E = !TellE
  fn deft(x: Overr) -> Int !TellE = x.v + tell()
}

alias Thunk[!e] = fn() -> Int !e

## The shape itself, with no alias anywhere. `run(x)` reads the projection as a
## callee's row and `t()` reads it as a pack, in one frame.
fn via[T: Runner](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !T.E) -> Int !T.E = run(x) + t()

## The same signature with the parameter written through a transparent alias.
## `Thunk[!T.E]` expands to `via`'s parameter type, so the two are one type and
## must be one behaviour (#351).
fn via_alias[T: Runner](x: T, t: Thunk[!T.E]) -> Int !T.E = run(x) + t()

## The projection forwarded one frame further out without ever being grounded:
## `relay`'s own slot fills `via`'s, and `t` is called on both sides of that
## hop. A pack that survives the forward and a pack rebuilt at each level are
## the same answer here; a pack replaced by a record on either hop is not.
fn relay[T: Runner](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !T.E) -> Int !T.E = via(x, t) + t()

## A written label beside the projection in the parameter's own row. The pack
## the call builds is one ground node consed over the projection's slot, and
## the closure reads through both halves.
fn beside[T: Runner](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !LogE !T.E) -> Int !LogE !T.E =
  run(x) + t() + log()

## The projection's pack carried into a primitive's closure argument: the use
## closure `bracket` applies has row `!e`, so `e` is instantiated to `!T.E` and
## the pack that fills its slot is this frame's projection slot forwarded whole.
fn via_bracketed[T: Runner](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !T.E) -> Int !T.E =
  bracket(0, y => (), y => via(x, t))

## The trait's **default** body reached through the projection. A default has
## the trait's own signature, so the whole trait-shaped evidence list rides to
## it and the impl's layout is re-selected inside the dictionary bridge rather
## than at a devirtualised call.
fn dbl[T: Runner](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !T.E) -> Int !T.E = twice(x) + t()

## A callee that binds nothing but its own effect variable. It is where the
## next two signatures put a row containing a projection, so what fills its one
## slot is a pack built out of the projection rather than the projection's slot
## forwarded whole.
fn thru(f: fn() -> Int !e) -> Int !e = f()

## A written label **beside** the projection, crossing that variable's slot.
## `!e` is instantiated to `!LogE !T.E`, so the pack is a ground node consed
## over the projection's slot and the node's `outer` field is where the bare
## record used to end up. A walk that stepped past the node found something
## that was not a pack node and was not the placeholder either, and the answer
## was a cast of one effect's record to another's rather than a miss.
fn label_over[T: Runner](x: T) -> Int !LogE !T.E = thru(() => run(x) + log())

## Two projections in one row, on two traits. Two non-ground atoms in one slot
## is `ev_append`'s case, which is the other way a record reaches a reader:
## joined into a chain rather than consed under a node.
fn two_traits[T: Runner, U: Other](a: T, b: U) -> Int !T.E !U.F =
  thru(() => run(a) + other(b))

## Two projections **on one trait**, read from inside a lambda, where both are
## addressed by the same `assoc_key` band. The lambda's own pack answers a
## non-label key with the whole chain, so both reads get a superset and both
## find their label's node in it.
fn two_subjects[T: Runner, U: Runner](a: T, b: U) -> Int !T.E !U.E = {
  let g = () => run(a) + run(b)
  g()
}

## Two members of one trait in one row, on one subject, read from inside a
## lambda the callee applies. `thru`'s `!e` is instantiated to `!T.E !T.F`, so
## one pack has to carry both members of one band.
fn both_members[T: Pair](x: T) -> Int !T.E !T.F = thru(() => lo(x) * 1000 + hi(x))

## The same row through `list.fold`, where the `!e` being instantiated belongs
## to a std signature's parameter rather than to anything this file wrote.
fn fold_members[T: Pair](x: T) -> Int !T.E !T.F =
  list.fold([1, 2], 0, (acc, _k) => acc + lo(x) * 1000 + hi(x))

## The two slots of one band split between a callee and this frame: `t` carries
## the second member's row into a function value, and the first is read here.
fn member_split[T: Pair](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !T.F) -> Int !T.E !T.F =
  lo(x) * 1000 + t()

## The control: a projection in a **return** row and nowhere else. This is the
## shape `assoc_effects.dawn` is made of, and the one that was right all along.
## It is here so that a repair which moves the parameter position by breaking
## the return position cannot look like a repair.
fn ret_only[T: Runner](x: T) -> Int !T.E = run(x)

## The defaulted member in the parameter position: the same read `via` does,
## over a slot whose row came from the trait's default (`ByDefault`) or from
## the impl's own overriding binding (`Overr`).
fn via_deft[T: Dflt](x: T, t: fn() -> Int !T.E) -> Int !T.E = deft(x) + t()

fn line_inline() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 3 }
  println(to_string(via(One { v: 100 }, () => ask() + 5)))
}

fn line_alias() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 7 }
  println(to_string(via_alias(One { v: 200 }, () => ask() * 2)))
}

fn line_two_labels() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 11 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 13 }
  println(to_string(via(One { v: 300 }, () => ask())))
  println(to_string(via(Two { v: 300 }, () => tell())))
}

fn line_fn_value() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 47 }
  let g: fn() -> Int !AskE = () => ask() * 3
  println(to_string(via(One { v: 900 }, g)))
}

fn line_bracketed() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 53 }
  println(to_string(via_bracketed(One { v: 1000 }, () => ask() + 59)))
}

fn line_pure() -> Unit !io = {
  println(to_string(via(Quiet { v: 400 }, () => 17)))
}

fn line_io() -> Unit !io = {
  println(to_string(via(Loud { v: 500 }, () => {
    println("io thunk")
    19
  })))
}

fn line_relay() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 23 }
  println(to_string(relay(One { v: 600 }, () => ask() + 29)))
}

fn line_beside() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 31 }
  with handle LogE { log() => 37 }
  println(to_string(beside(One { v: 700 }, () => ask() + log())))
}

fn line_default() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 41 }
  println(to_string(dbl(One { v: 800 }, () => ask() + 43)))
}

fn line_label_over() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 61 }
  with handle LogE { log() => 67 }
  println(to_string(label_over(One { v: 1100 })))
}

fn line_two_traits() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 71 }
  with handle LogE { log() => 73 }
  println(to_string(two_traits(One { v: 1200 }, Two { v: 1300 })))
}

fn line_two_subjects() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 79 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 83 }
  println(to_string(two_subjects(One { v: 1400 }, Two { v: 1500 })))
}

fn line_both_members() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 97 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 101 }
  println(to_string(both_members(Ping { v: 2 })))
  println(to_string(both_members(Pong { v: 2 })))
}

fn line_fold_members() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 113 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 127 }
  println(to_string(fold_members(Ping { v: 3 })))
}

fn line_member_split() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 107 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 109 }
  println(to_string(member_split(Ping { v: 5 }, () => tell() + 1)))
}

fn line_ret_only() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 89 }
  println(to_string(ret_only(One { v: 1600 })))
}

fn line_defaulted() -> Unit !io = {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 131 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 137 }
  println(to_string(via_deft(ByDefault { v: 1700 }, () => ask() + 1)))
  println(to_string(via_deft(Overr { v: 1800 }, () => tell() + 2)))
}

pub fn main() -> Unit !io = {
  println("-- a projection in a parameter row, written inline --")
  line_inline()
  println("-- the same row written through a transparent alias --")
  line_alias()
  println("-- one signature instantiated at two different labels --")
  line_two_labels()
  println("-- a named value crossing into the projection's slot --")
  line_fn_value()
  println("-- and the pack carried into a primitive's use closure --")
  line_bracketed()
  println("-- a binding that is pure: the empty pack, and the control --")
  line_pure()
  println("-- a binding that is io: the other empty pack --")
  line_io()
  println("-- forwarded through a second signature without grounding --")
  line_relay()
  println("-- a written label beside the projection in one row --")
  line_beside()
  println("-- and the trait default body, through the dictionary bridge --")
  line_default()
  println("-- a label beside the projection, crossing a variable's slot --")
  line_label_over()
  println("-- two projections in one row, on two traits --")
  line_two_traits()
  println("-- two projections on one trait, read inside a lambda --")
  line_two_subjects()
  println("-- two members of one trait, one subject, read inside a lambda --")
  line_both_members()
  println("-- the same two members through list.fold's parameter row --")
  line_fold_members()
  println("-- one member into a function value while the frame reads the other --")
  line_member_split()
  println("-- and the control: a projection in a return row and nowhere else --")
  line_ret_only()
  println("-- a defaulted member taken, and a defaulted member overridden --")
  line_defaulted()
}

# The assertion lane. `scripts/effect-evidence-contract/run.sh` runs these on
# the JVM in seconds and without a C compiler; the transcript above is what the
# native differential compares byte for byte. Same shapes, two readers: an
# `assert` says what the number means, and the `.expect` file is the only check
# that can see the *order* two closures ran in -- which is the whole of what
# `line_io` records.
test "a projection in a parameter row reaches the closure's pack" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 3 }
  assert via(One { v: 100 }, () => ask() + 5) == 111
}

test "the alias spelling is the same type and the same answer" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 7 }
  assert via_alias(One { v: 200 }, () => ask() * 2) == 221
}

test "two subjects put two different labels in the same slot" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 11 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 13 }
  assert via(One { v: 300 }, () => ask()) == 322
  assert via(Two { v: 300 }, () => tell()) == 326
}

test "a written row crosses into the projection's slot by assignability" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 47 }
  let g: fn() -> Int !AskE = () => ask() * 3
  assert via(One { v: 900 }, g) == 1088
}

test "a primitive's use closure carries the projection's pack" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 53 }
  assert via_bracketed(One { v: 1000 }, () => ask() + 59) == 1165
}

test "a pure binding is the empty pack" {
  assert via(Quiet { v: 400 }, () => 17) == 417
}

test "the projection survives a frame that only forwards it" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 23 }
  assert relay(One { v: 600 }, () => ask() + 29) == 727
}

test "a written label beside the projection is a node over its pack" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 31 }
  with handle LogE { log() => 37 }
  assert beside(One { v: 700 }, () => ask() + log()) == 836
}

test "a trait default body reads the projection through the bridge" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 41 }
  assert dbl(One { v: 800 }, () => ask() + 43) == 1766
}

test "a label consed over the projection keeps what is under the node" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 61 }
  with handle LogE { log() => 67 }
  assert label_over(One { v: 1100 }) == 1228
}

test "two projections in one slot are joined, not dropped" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 71 }
  with handle LogE { log() => 73 }
  assert two_traits(One { v: 1200 }, Two { v: 1300 }) == 2644
}

test "two projections on one trait answer with their own labels" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 79 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 83 }
  assert two_subjects(One { v: 1400 }, Two { v: 1500 }) == 3062
}

test "two members of one trait answer with their own bindings" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 97 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 101 }
  # Ping binds E to AskE and F to TellE; Pong binds them the other way, so the
  # two answers are each other's digits and a swapped slot cannot pass both
  assert both_members(Ping { v: 2 }) == 99103
  assert both_members(Pong { v: 2 }) == 103099
}

test "two members of one trait survive a std parameter's row" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 113 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 127 }
  assert fold_members(Ping { v: 3 }) == 232260
}

test "one band's two slots split between a callee and the frame" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 107 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 109 }
  assert member_split(Ping { v: 5 }, () => tell() + 1) == 112110
}

test "a projection in a return row is what it always was" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 89 }
  assert ret_only(One { v: 1600 }) == 1689
}

test "a defaulted member reads through the default's label" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 131 }
  # ByDefault writes no binding: the trait's `= !AskE` is its row, so both
  # the impl body's `ask()` and the thunk's answer through AskE's handler
  assert via_deft(ByDefault { v: 1700 }, () => ask() + 1) == 1963
}

test "an impl's own binding overrides the default" {
  with handle AskE { ask() => 131 }
  with handle TellE { tell() => 137 }
  # Overr binds `= !TellE`: a default applied over the written binding, or a
  # materialization that ignores the declared row, would reach for AskE's
  # handler here and the sum says so
  assert via_deft(Overr { v: 1800 }, () => tell() + 2) == 2076
}
